# KAI line-ending policy — everything is LF, everywhere.
#
# Why: the in-app AI edit tools match `old_string` byte-exactly. A multi-line
# anchor written with \n can never match a CRLF working tree (single-line
# edits keep working, which makes the failure look random). `eol=lf` forces LF
# on checkout on every OS and overrides core.autocrlf on Windows; `text=auto`
# normalizes to LF on add, so the repo can never regress to CRLF.
#
# Working-tree corruption STILL happens (PowerShell line surgery writes
# CRLF: [IO.File]::WriteAllLines / Set-Content / Out-File), and git status
# shows NOTHING for it — add normalizes, so only the byte-exact edit anchors
# notice. Check with `pnpm eol:check`, repair with `pnpm eol:fix`
# (scripts/eol.mjs — the SAFE stage-first recipe). NEVER repair by hand with
# `git checkout -- <file>`: on unstaged work that restores the last COMMITTED
# content and silently destroys your edits (it cost a full extraction session
# once), and on EOL-only diffs it is a no-op anyway until the file is deleted.
# The test suite's eolHygiene gate fails if the tree ever regresses.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries — never normalize, never diff-merge.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.webp binary
*.bmp  binary
*.tiff binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.eot  binary
*.mp3  binary
*.mp4  binary
*.wav  binary
*.ogg  binary
*.flac binary
*.mov  binary
*.webm binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.tgz  binary
*.tar  binary
*.7z   binary
*.rar  binary
*.exe  binary
*.dll  binary
*.so   binary
*.dylib binary
*.a    binary
*.lib  binary
*.wasm binary
*.node binary
*.pdf  binary
